# Architecture constraints

## Shared core + swappable frontends
Business logic and sync engine live in a **framework-agnostic core layer**.
UI layers (web, Flutter, Java Swing/JavaFX) are thin wrappers over the core.
Never put domain logic in a UI component.

## Core responsibilities
- Local database (workspace snapshot)
- Sync engine: named sync sets with independent scheduling + status tracking
- Domain model: milestone → task hierarchy, context selection, release notes
- Write-back queue: stage changes, hold for user approval, then push to ClickUp API

## Local copy
The local DB is the source of truth for reads. All navigation and display goes
through the local copy — never a live ClickUp API call for read operations.

## ClickUp API
Used only for:
1. Initial full workspace pull (one-time)
2. Incremental sync (delta fetch for tasks)
3. Approved write-back pushes

## Performance target
All reads from the local copy must complete in ≤ 100 ms. Any query that cannot
meet this must be pre-computed / indexed at sync time, not at read time.
